ucing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162007 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162007 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form Using Orthogonal Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7a60cb12-dd77-5740-b1c2-9fd58434b948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162854 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162854 #edb/ref 1061 30542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162854 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a819afd-fa45-5c12-8319-bbdf2452f54b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162854 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162892 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162892 #edb/ref 1061 30849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1325a77d-0093-5d23-9495-014e8f550773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162893 #edb/ref 1061 30850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "459f8fc0-d5b4-5716-9c7e-669c15d5ffdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162894 #edb/ref 1061 30851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162894 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65c62c86-c42d-58f2-9237-353231d4f733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162894 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163229 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a quadratic form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. As we know, any symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized as follows:\n\n$$\nD = P^T \\! AP,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is an orthogonal matrix and $D$ is a diagonal matrix that contains eigenvalues of $A$ on the main diagonal. \n\nNow, substituting the change of variable $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}$ into our quadratic form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}\\,AP)y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,Dy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a canonical form. \n\nAs a result, using the orthogonal diagonalization of $A,$ we can find the canonical form of $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the canonical form of \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/43/43e0dc28161c0564a75f6ea2dc7f0e09ad74dffae05b864e13c61330221f2983.svg)\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/c9/c93cabd6a18df429b5aad99815b23e337c17b88a1c271dd17344631dfc5f8ebb.svg)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 - y_2^2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163229 #edb/ref 1065 4888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163229 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "26652f7d-7ad4-54a0-b4a6-5d9faafa8403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163229 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364544 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364544 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7869b9c-6fbb-54b1-92e1-8d96639ffbe6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364544 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364545 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2f96ff58-af7b-58d2-b1bc-1f8c4ba99615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364546 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "669f4399-98d0-58cb-b5ca-020a4654ee8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364547 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364547 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364547 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43a786aa-dee4-53e1-9bc6-44e46ac40c31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364548 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364548 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364548 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c4f840fe-347c-55e1-bbf6-32e391995c9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364549 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8b0a414-8efb-5f7c-aa2d-597889961eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364550 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6efe3c1a-3df7-5ca6-9447-5ad51ed877d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364558 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ebe6e82-d911-58c0-a13c-cf64821873f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364558 #edb/ref 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364559 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2252911e-75f8-59ae-b664-6789cb01b1ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364560 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364560 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364560