 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573933 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d75d5c4d-ca96-55cd-81ad-3a4ef4cad6d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573933 #edb/ref 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573949 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04ddc6fd-b21b-52ad-8cd2-7aed3b7a2fb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573949 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186364558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573951 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573951 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e3c8864-c3d0-5194-8e33-93ae8cadd5bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573951 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186404766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573954 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573954 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a2e605c-b1b4-5c33-9bf9-2d4531698d99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573954 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573969 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573969 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ba26dcb-ada9-5956-bd40-70458277cdef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573969 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186395003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573975 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573975 #edb/ref 1062 #